# Typesense has two index paths, and they used to disagree

`src/lib/typesense-query.ts` writes to Typesense from two places, and they are
not interchangeable:

- **`upsertPostToTypeSense(resource, action)`** — the singular save path, fired
  by the `content-resource-index-requested` Inngest function. Writes the full
  document: `summary`, `tags`, `parentResources`, `published_at_timestamp`.
- **`indexAllContentToTypeSense(resources, deleteFirst)`** — the bulk path used
  by `pnpm index-typesense` (`src/scripts/index-content.ts`). Writes a narrower
  document with `action: 'emplace'`, so fields it omits survive on existing
  docs; fields it *does* send overwrite whatever the save path wrote.

Until 2026-09-02 the bulk path set `type: resource.type` while the save path
preferred `fields.postType`. A bulk reindex therefore silently flattened every
skill post from `type: 'skill'` to `type: 'post'`, breaking the `type:=skill`
facet on `/posts` and `/q`. Both now go through one `deriveResourceType`
helper. **Any field added to one path must be checked against the other** —
emplace makes divergence silent, not loud.

### `fields.type` is not a resource type

The save path's original fallback chain ended `fields.postType → fields.type →
resource.type`, and the first attempt at sharing it carried `fields.type`
along. That is wrong, and the mistake is easy to repeat: on a `list`,
`fields.type` is the list's *flavour* — `nextUp | tutorial | workshop`
(`ListTypeSchema`, `src/lib/lists.ts:12`) — not its resource type. Two live
lists carry `fields.type: 'workshop'`, including
`ai-engineering-crash-course~pniml`, and `getResourcePath('workshop', slug)`
routes to `/workshops/<slug>` where a list belongs at `/<slug>`. Indexed type
feeds hit URLs, so this class of error produces broken links, not just a wrong
facet.

`deriveResourceType` now lives in its own dependency-free module
(`src/lib/typesense-resource-type.ts`) with `typesense-resource-type.test.ts`
pinning the list cases — `typesense-query.ts` drags in the db and next-auth,
so a helper inside it cannot be unit-tested.

## Collections

`content_production` (live) and `content_joel_dev` sit in the same Typesense
cloud instance, selected by `NEXT_PUBLIC_TYPESENSE_COLLECTION_NAME`. Local
`.env` points at the dev collection, so a bulk reindex of production means
overriding that env var — there is no other guard.

## The bulk script does not run outside Next

`pnpm index-typesense` fails on plain `tsx`, for two reasons that have nothing
to do with Typesense:

1. `server-only` is not an installed package — Next aliases it internally. Node
   resolves it and throws `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND`.
2. `index-content.ts` imports `ai-coding-dictionary-typesense.ts` at module
   scope, which pulls in the browser `typesense-instantsearch-adapter`; its CJS
   default export is not a constructor under Node ESM.

Both are import-graph problems, so they bite even when the run never touches
the dictionary branch. Reindexing a subset today means a throwaway script that
imports only `indexAllContentToTypeSense`.

## Direct `fields` writes never reindex

Images, and any other `fields` JSON written straight to MySQL rather than
through `updatePost`, do not fire `CONTENT_RESOURCE_INDEX_REQUESTED`, so search
keeps serving the old value indefinitely. `/posts` is entirely Typesense-backed
(`posts/page.tsx` → `(search)/q/_components/search` → `hit.tsx` rendering
`hit.image` or `bg-stripes`), so a stale index reads as missing or outdated
artwork on the catalog. See [[add-skill-post-icon-field]].

## Reindexing is not enough — bust the Next caches too

Confirmed live 2026-09-02. After Typesense held the correct images, the
production page still served the old ones, and kept flip-flopping between
old and new as different edges expired. Two Next layers sit in front of the
index, both keyed to nothing Typesense knows about:

- `getCachedPostsGraph` — `unstable_cache`, `revalidate: 3600`, tag
  `posts-graph` (`src/lib/posts-graph.ts`). Carries `image` per node, and
  survives page regeneration, so a freshly regenerated page can still hold
  hour-old artwork.
- `/posts` itself — `dynamic = 'force-static'` with `revalidate = 3600`, and
  its hits are server-rendered by `InstantSearchNext`, so the SSR markup
  bakes in whatever the index held at generation time.

`curl -sD- https://www.aihero.dev/posts` and grepping the Cloudinary `v…`
version segment tells you what the edge is actually serving; `x-vercel-cache`
plus `age` tells you why. The fix is
`GET /api/revalidate?tag=posts-graph&secret=$INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY` (the route
only accepts tags, never paths). Expect one `STALE` response while the
background regeneration runs, then `HIT` with fresh URLs. **Any Typesense
reindex that changes what `/posts` displays must be followed by that call.**
